= Wave 1 compiled context =

'''State: Phase 2 — Context compilation + writing.'''

This page normalizes the Wave 1 research state into one semantic vocabulary before article prose is written.

== Dependency order ==

Wave 1 is compiled from epistemic primitives outward:

<pre>
Belief ─┐
        ├→ Knowledge
Evidence├→ Justification
        └→ Epistemic status
             ↑
        Uncertainty

Evidence → Empirical evidence
Evidence → Provenance → Verification
Verification ↔ Validation

Scientific method
 ├→ Empirical evidence
 ├→ Measurement → Accuracy and precision
 ├→ Scientific model → Explanation
 ├→ Falsifiability → Counterexample
 ├→ Reproducibility
 └→ Replication
</pre>

This ordering is editorial, not ontological.

== Normalized definitions ==

=== Belief ===
A mental/propositional attitude of taking something to be the case or true. Belief may be dispositional or occurrent and can be modeled categorically or by graded credence.

=== Knowledge ===
An epistemic achievement/state in which a subject gets at truth in the relevant way. Propositional knowledge normally involves truth and belief, but justified true belief is not sufficient by simple definition and no reductive analysis is universally accepted.

=== Epistemology ===
The philosophical study of knowledge and related epistemic phenomena: belief, justification, evidence, rationality, ignorance, skepticism, inquiry, credence, testimony, and epistemic norms.

=== Evidence ===
Material that bears epistemically on a claim, proposition, hypothesis, or belief—affecting support, justification, confirmation, or rational credence. What counts as evidence and how evidence supports claims is theory- and domain-sensitive.

=== Empirical evidence ===
Evidence grounded in observation, experiment, measurement, or experience of the world. Empirical evidence is one kind of evidence and is not equivalent to raw data, causal proof, or all scientific evidence.

=== Justification ===
The epistemic standing of a belief as appropriately supported, rational, warranted, or held on good grounds. Competing theories disagree about whether justification depends on accessible reasons/evidence, reliable processes, coherence, foundations, or other conditions.

=== Uncertainty ===
Incomplete determination concerning a proposition, state, parameter, model, measurement, outcome, or future event. The term covers several distinct forms, including epistemic uncertainty, aleatory variability, model uncertainty, semantic/vagueness uncertainty, and metrological measurement uncertainty.

=== Epistemic status ===
A family term for epistemically evaluative properties or standings of beliefs/claims, such as justification, knowledge, warrant, certainty, reliability, safety, sensitivity, defeat-resistance, or degrees of support. It is not one universal ordered scale.

=== Provenance ===
Information about origin, custody, production, transformation, derivation, and responsible agents/activities associated with an object, data item, record, claim, or artifact.

=== Verification ===
Checking or confirming, using appropriate evidence or formal procedure, that specified requirements, properties, calculations, implementations, or claims are satisfied. The precise object and criterion of verification are domain-specific.

=== Validation ===
Evaluation or confirmation that something is adequate, valid, or fit for an intended use, target, application, interpretation, or empirical domain. Validation is domain-specific and is not equivalent to verification or proof.

=== Falsifiability ===
A property of a claim/theory under which some possible observation, counterinstance, or basic statement would conflict with it. Logical falsifiability must be distinguished from the methodological decision that an actual observation has falsified a theory.

=== Scientific method ===
The family of systematic practices used in science to observe, measure, model, hypothesize, experiment, infer, test, compare, revise, and communicate claims. There is no single universal step sequence sufficient for all sciences.

=== Scientific model ===
A representation used in scientific inquiry to describe, explain, predict, explore, simulate, infer about, or understand a target system, phenomenon, data set, or theory.

=== Measurement ===
A structured process for obtaining a result/representation concerning a property or quantity through interaction, comparison, modeling, calibration, reference systems, and inference. A measurement result is not merely an instrument reading.

=== Accuracy and precision ===
Measurement accuracy concerns closeness to an appropriate reference/true value; measurement precision concerns closeness of agreement among replicate measurements under specified conditions. High precision does not imply high accuracy.

=== Reproducibility ===
A field-dependent term for the ability to obtain consistent results under specified repeat conditions. In the National Academies 2019 convention used as Wave 1's main science distinction, reproducibility means obtaining consistent computational results using the same data, code/methods, and analysis conditions.

=== Replication ===
A new investigation/study aimed at the same or a closely related scientific question using newly obtained data or observations. In the National Academies convention, replicability concerns consistency across such studies.

=== Explanation ===
An account that makes a phenomenon, fact, event, result, or pattern intelligible by showing why or how it obtains. Explanations may be causal, mechanistic, nomological, statistical, unificationist, model-based, mathematical, pragmatic, or other context-specific forms.

=== Counterexample ===
A case that satisfies a general claim's hypotheses/conditions while making its conclusion false. A valid counterexample refutes a universal claim, but isolated contrary observations do not automatically refute probabilistic/statistical claims.

== Required non-equivalences ==

<pre>
belief ≠ knowledge
belief ≠ credence
knowledge ≠ justified true belief by simple definition
evidence ≠ truth
evidence ≠ proof
data ≠ evidence without relevance/inference context
empirical evidence ≠ all evidence
justification ≠ truth
uncertainty ≠ error
uncertainty ≠ probability
epistemic status ≠ confidence
epistemic status ≠ truth value
epistemic status ≠ lifecycle state
provenance ≠ citation
provenance ≠ truth
verification ≠ validation
verification ≠ proof
validation ≠ certainty
falsifiable ≠ false
unfalsified ≠ verified
scientific method ≠ one universal recipe
scientific model ≠ scientific theory
scientific model ≠ simulation
instrument reading ≠ measurement result
accuracy ≠ precision
reproducibility ≠ replication
reproducibility ≠ correctness
replication failure ≠ automatic falsification
explanation ≠ description
explanation ≠ prediction
counterexample ≠ anecdote
</pre>

